    "created_at": "2017-01-23T23:02:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-01-23T23:02:35Z",
    "body": "There are actually two Web IDL callbacks that are underdefined here:\r\n\r\n- (A) the result of \"get the current value of the event handler H\" is a function compiled from a string or set via the IDL attribute.\r\n- (B) tone created by \"the event handler processing algorithm\", which *calls* the first one, and is a wrapper that is stored in the event listeners list.\r\n\r\n(A) has the problem listed here that, when it is compiled from a string, it should be an IDL callback. This is easy to fix.\r\n\r\n(B) has a separate problem in that it isn't very well-defined. The spec says\r\n\r\n> When an event handler H of an element or object T implementing the EventTarget interface is first set to a non-null value, the user agent must append an event listener to the list of event listeners associated with T with type set to the event handler event type corresponding to H and callback set to the event handler processing algorithm defined below. \r\n\r\nBut here \"callback\" is supposed to be an EventListener instance, not a series of steps that take as input an event handler and an event object :(.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure how to get from the series of steps to the callback instance. Notably, I can't figure out what its callback context should be, or what effect setting it would have.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
